+## 3. Task C — two independent hull routes and their agreement
+
+**Route 1 (pairing / Gram).** With $A$ an $\mathbb F_q$-basis of $C$ (flat $R_n$ coordinates)
+form the restricted Gram matrix $M_{ij}=\mathfrak b_s(x_i,x_j)$ — note $\mathfrak b_s$
+already contains $\sigma_s$ and the transpose, so $M=\sigma_s(A)\,G\,A^{\!\top}$ — and take
+
+$$\dim\operatorname{Hull}_s(C)=\dim C-\operatorname{rank}M,\qquad
+\operatorname{Hull}_s(C)=\{c\in C: c\text{ is }M\text{-orthogonal to all of }C\}.$$
+
+**Route 2 (Morita / annihilator).** With $g_1,\dots,g_m$ right-ideal generators of $C$,
+
+$$W'=W(C^{\perp_s})=\bigcap_i\ker\theta_s(\operatorname{rev}g_i),\qquad
+\operatorname{Hull}_s(C)=I_{W\cap W'},\quad \dim\operatorname{Hull}_s(C)=2\dim_{\mathbb F_q}(W\cap W')
+$$
+
+(Stage 2C Thm 3.8, Stage 3A Thm 2.2 and Thm 3.3).
+
+**Result (`[EXH]`, every code of every family, every $s$).** The two routes agree **both
+in dimension and as subspaces** (compared by rref key) on
+
+* $(4,1,1)$: $7$ codes; $(4,1,2)$: $33$; $(4,2,1)$: $33$; $(8,1,2)$: $93$;
+  $(4,1,3)$: $343$; $(9,1,2)$: $144$; $(4,2,2)$: $1221$ — **0 disagreements on either
+  metric**, and additionally $\dim C+\dim C^{\perp_s}=4tn$ on every code
+  ($C^{\perp_s}$ computed independently as an annihilator), 0 violations.
+
+A third, annihilator-based route is used inside Route 2 (the kernels
+$\ker\theta_s(\operatorname{rev}g_i)$ are computed as annihilator kernels, and the local
+dual in §6 is computed both by the local pairing and by the Morita annihilator — the two
+agree, `[EXH]`, in every split case).
+
+> **Repaired precondition (Stage-3B debugging note).** The Stage-2C helpers
+> `mu_local`/`mu_components` compute $\dim W$ as the $\mathbb F_q$-rank of the *list they
+> are handed*; they must therefore be given an $\mathbb F_q$-basis, not a $B$-generating
+> set. Stage 2C always passed a basis, so the precondition was invisible there. Passing
+> $W[\text{gens}]$ silently under-counts $\dim W$ and produced wrong $\mu$ and
+> route-disagreements at $(4,1,2)$. Fixed by routing through an explicit basis. The
+> counterexample worth remembering: $W=\langle e_1,Xe_1,(1+X)e_2\rangle$ over
+> $\mathbb F_4[X]/\langle X-1\rangle^2$ is generated by $\{(X,0),(0,1+X)\}$, whose
+> $\mathbb F_4$-span is only $2$-dimensional.
