+| **T-151** | **C3 — CRT components and the Galois permutation.** In the **split** regime let $\pi$ (from $\sigma_s$: $\zeta\mapsto\zeta^{p^s}$) and $\rho$ (from $\operatorname{rev}$: $\zeta\mapsto\zeta^{-1}$) be the induced permutations of the components, and $\tau=\pi^{-1}\circ\rho$ (characterised by $\zeta_{\tau(j)}^{p^s}=\zeta_j^{-1}$). Then $(C^{\perp_s})_j=(C_{\tau(j)})^{\perp_s}$, $\mathrm{Hull}_s(C)_j=C_j\cap(C_{\tau(j)})^{\perp_s}$ and $\dim\mathrm{Hull}=\sum_j\dim\bigl(C_j\cap(C_{\tau(j)})^{\perp_s}\bigr)$. **The hull decomposes componentwise ($\tau=\mathrm{id}$) iff $n\mid p^s+1$.** When $\tau\ne\mathrm{id}$ the naive formula $\sum_j\dim\mathrm{Hull}_s(C_j)$ fails: at $(4,1,3)$, $s=0$ ($\tau=[0,2,1]$) for 9 of 20 codes, and at $(4,2,3)$, $s=0$ for 1 of 20; witness $g=\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}2+X&3+2X^2\\1+2X+2X^2&1+X+X^2\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$ has $\dim\mathrm{Hull}_0(C)=2$ but $\sum_j\dim\mathrm{Hull}_0(C_j)=0$. Chain-regime cases are **trivial** (one component) and wild cases have **no** decomposition; both are stated rather than silently skipped | **split** regime: $p\nmid n$ and all $n$-th roots of unity in $\mathbb F_q$ | Stage 3A Thm 3.5 | T-110, T-126, T-127 | **`PROVED IN PHASE 3 STAGE 3A`** | component formulas on 20 codes × all components at 10 pairs; $\tau$ printed per pair with the $n\mid p^s+1$ test; naive formula fails exactly when $\tau\ne\mathrm{id}$ | §3.3 |
