+
+\subsection{The certification table}
+
+Table~\ref{tab:cert} is the complete output of the certification stage: all
+$28$ certified codes, reproduced verbatim from the computation log
+(\filepath{04_COMPUTATION/phase7_extended_search_log.txt}, Part~6). The
+columns M1, M2, M3 record the three independent distance methods of
+Section~\ref{sec:cert}. At most two of the three are run for any one code:
+a dash in the M1 column means M1 could not complete within its enumeration
+cap, in which case the support search M2 is run instead; ``n/a'' in the M2
+column means M2 was not run, because M1 had already finished and M2 is run
+only when M1 cannot. Thus exactly one of M1 and M2 carries the exact distance
+for each code. M3 runs for all $28$ codes and is read as follows. It searches
+in increasing symplectic weight from $1$, so a returned value is the true
+minimum: an entry $2$ or $3$ is therefore M3 \emph{independently determining}
+the distance exactly, and this happens for $11$ of the $28$ codes. A ``$>2$''
+(or ``$>3$'') entry means M3 reached weight $2$ (resp. $3$) without finding a
+vector, i.e. it certifies $d\ge3$ (resp. $d\ge4$) as a \emph{lower} bound
+only; this happens for the remaining $17$ codes. So the cross-check supplied by
+M3 is an exact independent determination for $11$ codes and a consistent proved
+lower bound for the other $17$ --- it is not uniform across the table, and the
+per-row entry, not a general rule, settles what M3 gave for a particular code.
+The column ``exact'' says yes when every method that completed agrees
+with the reported value, the stabilizer is $\F_p$-abelian, and $K$ and $c$
+recompute from $k$ and $h_E$.
